---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SectionNav from '../../components/SectionNav.astro';
---
<Base title="Ratings" description="How editorial dots, interest tags, and visit rank are calculated on Vietnam Guide." path="/about/ratings">
  <article class="wrap section">
    <h1>Ratings</h1>
    <SectionNav
      label="Ratings sections"
      items={[
        { id: 'dots', label: 'Editorial dots' },
        { id: 'interests', label: 'Interest tags' },
        { id: 'rank', label: 'Visit rank' },
        { id: 'recommended', label: 'Recommended order' },
      ]}
    />
    <div class="reading">
    <section id="dots" class="place-section">
    <h2>Editorial dots</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>5 — worth building a day around</li>
      <li>4 — worth a special trip in the corridor</li>
      <li>3 — worth it if you are already nearby</li>
      <li>2 — a short stop or a niche interest</li>
      <li>1 — listed for completeness</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Dots change when the place changes. They do not rise because a page got traffic.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="interests" class="place-section">
    <h2>Interest tags</h2>
    <p>Each place has weights for food, beaches, caves, and the other trip interests. Those weights order a list when you are choosing by interest. They are set by editors.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="rank" class="place-section">
    <h2>Visit rank</h2>
    <p>A public label such as “most visited in the central coast” needs 28 days and 500 sessions. Until then the chip stays off. The 90-day score counts visits, time on the page, map opens, saves, and clicks through to directions. Affiliate clicks are not included.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="recommended" class="place-section">
    <h2>Recommended order</h2>
    <p>Lists default to dots first. Traffic only breaks a tie, and only after a place qualifies. A sponsored story cannot sit in this order.</p>
    </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
